From: hurrhnn <hurrhnn@icmp.kr>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 00:30:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] xfrm: keep one egress flow hash per SA

An outbound SA numbers its packets in xfrm_output_one(), before they
reach the qdisc and the tx queue selection of the egress device. The
peer drops a packet whose sequence number has fallen behind its replay
window, so the packets of an SA have to leave close to the order in
which they were numbered.

The transformed packet keeps the skb->hash of the inner packet, the
receive hash of a forwarded packet or the socket hash of a local one.
Flow hashing qdiscs and the hash based tx queue selection of
skb_tx_hash() and XPS therefore still see the inner flows and split one
SA by them. fq_codel puts every inner flow of the SA into its own
bucket, and its DRR and new flow scheduling send packets with newer
sequence numbers ahead of an older backlog. With a software ESP SA,
every TCP retransmission of an upload through the tunnel matched a
packet that the peer had dropped as too old for its replay window, and
the drops stopped when all packets of the SA were kept in one fq_codel
bucket.

Clearing the hash, so that the flow dissector hashes the outer header,
is not enough for IPv6: tunnel mode copies the flow label of an inner
IPv6 packet into the outer header, and the dissector stops at a
non-zero flow label.

Set a software L4 hash computed from the state instead, from its
addresses, protocol and SPI, for ESP and AH states, the states with
replay protection. It is set once the outer header has been added and
before the packet is numbered, so it is also carried by packets that
complete through asynchronous crypto, by ESP GSO and crypto offload
packets, which are encrypted after the qdisc, and by the GSO segments
made from a packet later on. With nested states the outermost one sets
the hash. Different SAs still hash apart.

The inner flows of one SA lose their fq_codel isolation from each
other. RFC 4301 section 4.1 already expects a sender to put traffic
classes that are scheduled apart on different SAs, because the replay
window of the receiver would discard the packets that fall behind.

A tx queue choice that does not use the hash, such as one made from
skb->priority or from the XPS map of the sending CPU, can still split
an SA. The slow path fragments of a locally generated packet take the
socket hash again in skb_set_owner_w().

Packet offload states skip this part of xfrm_output_one() and are not
changed.

Signed-off-by: hurrhnn <hurrhnn@icmp.kr>
---
--- a/net/xfrm/xfrm_output.c
+++ b/net/xfrm/xfrm_output.c
@@ -525,6 +525,41 @@ unlock:
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(xfrm_dev_packet_state_check);
 
+/* The packets of an SA share its sequence number space and have to leave
+ * in the order in which they are numbered. Hash them by the SA, not by
+ * the inner flow, so that flow hashing qdiscs and hash based tx queue
+ * selection do not reorder them across the peer's replay window.
+ */
+static void xfrm_set_flow_hash(struct sk_buff *skb,
+			       const struct xfrm_state *x)
+{
+	struct flow_keys keys;
+
+	if (!(x->type->flags & XFRM_TYPE_REPLAY_PROT))
+		return;
+
+	memset(&keys, 0, sizeof(keys));
+	keys.basic.ip_proto = x->id.proto;
+	keys.ports.ports = x->id.spi;
+
+	switch (x->props.family) {
+	case AF_INET:
+		keys.control.addr_type = FLOW_DISSECTOR_KEY_IPV4_ADDRS;
+		keys.basic.n_proto = htons(ETH_P_IP);
+		keys.addrs.v4addrs.src = x->props.saddr.a4;
+		keys.addrs.v4addrs.dst = x->id.daddr.a4;
+		break;
+	case AF_INET6:
+		keys.control.addr_type = FLOW_DISSECTOR_KEY_IPV6_ADDRS;
+		keys.basic.n_proto = htons(ETH_P_IPV6);
+		keys.addrs.v6addrs.src = x->props.saddr.in6;
+		keys.addrs.v6addrs.dst = x->id.daddr.in6;
+		break;
+	}
+
+	__skb_set_sw_hash(skb, flow_hash_from_keys(&keys), true);
+}
+
 static int xfrm_output_one(struct sk_buff *skb, int err)
 {
 	struct dst_entry *dst = skb_dst(skb);
@@ -557,6 +592,8 @@ static int xfrm_output_one(struct sk_buf
 			goto error_nolock;
 		}
 
+		xfrm_set_flow_hash(skb, x);
+
 		err = xfrm_dev_packet_state_check(x, skb);
 		if (err)
 			goto error_nolock;
